# Citation File Format 1.2.0. What a reader citing this work should put in a
# bibliography, in the one file the tools that build bibliographies look for.
#
# Two fields a citation file usually carries are absent, and the absence is the
# point rather than an omission to be filled in later by whoever notices.
#
#   version and date-released
#       No release has been cut. A version here would name one that does not
#       exist, and a date would be the day somebody edited this file rather
#       than the day anything was released. Issue #99 is where both arrive,
#       together with the identifier minted for that release.
#
#   an identifier for the author
#       No `orcid` under the author below. An author identifier is one of the
#       two things issue #99 waits on, and an empty one written in now would be
#       a key a bibliography tool reads and resolves to nothing, which is worse
#       than the key being absent.
#
#   license
#       This tree carries two sets of terms, AGPL-3.0 over the code and CC BY
#       4.0 over the theory documents, the reference values and the prose. The
#       code half has an SPDX identifier this tree has not chosen between:
#       nothing here says whether it is version 3 only or version 3 or later,
#       because the notice that would say so is applied nowhere, and writing
#       either one into this file would decide it by accident in the file
#       furthest from where such a thing is decided. LICENSE, LICENSE-CONTENT
#       and the License section of README.md are the authority meanwhile.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: rechenstrasse
message: >-
  If you use this software, cite it. Cite the version you ran rather than the
  project in general, because a result depends on the version that produced it.
  The Citing this work section of README.md says how, and says what to do until
  a release carries an identifier of its own.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Lehnen
    given-names: Nils
repository-code: https://github.com/iderex/rechenstrasse
abstract: >-
  A pipeline from a proposed gravitational action to field equations,
  post-Newtonian parameters and cosmological perturbation equations. It works
  symbolically, refuses by name the theory families it does not cover, and
  records what a run needs so that the same input reproduces the same output.
keywords:
  - general relativity
  - modified gravity
  - post-Newtonian
  - scalar-tensor
  - computer algebra
  - reproducibility
